  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Houston building codes are the city's adopted versions of the International Code Council model codes, amended for the things Houston deals with every year: heavy rain, flooding, hurricane winds and relentless humidity. If you are building inside city limits, you will work with the <a href="https://www.houstonpermittingcenter.org">Houston Permitting Center</a>. Here is what the codes cover, what is unique about building in Houston, and where to find the official rules.</p>

<h2>What the codes cover</h2>
<p>For a new house, Houston's codes set minimum standards for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Structure:</strong> foundations, framing, roof assemblies and wind resistance</li>
<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> wiring, circuits, grounding and protective devices</li>
<li><strong>Plumbing and mechanical:</strong> water, drain, gas, HVAC and ventilation</li>
<li><strong>Fire and life safety:</strong> smoke and CO alarms, egress, garage separation</li>
<li><strong>Energy:</strong> insulation, air sealing, windows and duct sealing</li>
</ul>
<p>Houston adopts the International Residential Code (IRC) for one- and two-family homes and the International Building Code (IBC) for commercial and larger multifamily buildings, along with companion codes and local amendments. You can read the adopted codes on the <a href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/codes/texas/houston-tx">Houston ICC building code site</a>. Editions change every few years, so confirm what is current before you design.</p>

<h2>What makes building in Houston different</h2>
<h3>Flooding and floodplain rules</h3>
<p>This is the big one. If your lot is in a mapped floodplain, the city requires the finished floor to be elevated above specified flood levels, and Houston tightened its floodplain rules after Hurricane Harvey. Expect a floodplain review, an elevation certificate, and possibly a design built on piers or a raised slab. Even outside mapped floodplains, plan for good lot drainage and a finished floor comfortably above the street.</p>
<h3>Wind</h3>
<p>Houston sits in a high-wind region, so codes require roof sheathing nailing, connectors and window ratings that resist hurricane-force gusts. Closer to the coast, the Texas Department of Insurance windstorm certification program applies in designated areas. Most of the city is outside that zone, but check if you are building toward Galveston Bay.</p>
<h3>No zoning, but plenty of rules</h3>
<p>Houston famously lacks traditional zoning. That does not mean anything goes. The city enforces development rules for lot size, setbacks and parking, and many neighborhoods have deed restrictions that can control lot use, home size, materials and even fence height. Deed restrictions are legally enforceable, and the city can help enforce some of them. Read them before you buy a lot.</p>
<h3>Heat and humidity</h3>
<p>Moisture management matters as much as insulation here. Proper flashing, vapor control, right-sized HVAC equipment and sealed ducts prevent mold and comfort problems. Our guide on <a href="/energy-efficient-home-building-in-the-texas-heat/">energy-efficient home building in the Texas heat</a> covers practical choices.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Pull your lot's flood zone and any elevation data before you finalize a floor plan. Raising a slab or switching to piers changes your foundation, stairs, driveway slope and budget, and it is far cheaper to plan for it than to discover it in plan review.</p></div>

<h2>The permitting process</h2>
<ol>
<li>Check deed restrictions, plat and floodplain status.</li>
<li>Prepare plans: site plan with drainage, floor plans, elevations, foundation and framing details, and energy compliance.</li>
<li>Submit through the Houston Permitting Center and respond to plan review comments.</li>
<li>Once approved, state-licensed electricians, plumbers and HVAC contractors pull trade permits.</li>
<li>Pass inspections at each stage, then the final.</li>
</ol>
<p>Fees and review times change, so check current figures with the Houston Permitting Center. Complete, clearly drawn plans move through review faster, and a designer who submits in Houston regularly will know the reviewers' common comments. If your lot is in unincorporated Harris County or a neighboring city, the rules and the permitting office are different.</p>

<h2>Inspections and common violations</h2>
<p>Typical inspections include plumbing under the slab, foundation, framing with rough trades, insulation and energy, and finals. The violations inspectors see most often in Houston include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Unpermitted work or additions</li>
<li>Poor drainage or grading, especially in flood-prone areas</li>
<li>Electrical and plumbing work that does not meet code</li>
<li>Missing smoke and CO alarms or other safety items</li>
</ul>
<p>Pre-inspect your own job before the city does. A plain-language reference like [[amz:books_code_check|Code Check]] helps, and a [[amz:moisture_meter|moisture meter]] is valuable in Houston humidity before insulation and drywall go up.</p>
<!--products:books_code_check,moisture_meter,dehumidifier|Helpful gear for a humid Houston build-->

<h2>If you rent while you build</h2>
<p>Many of our readers rent in Houston while they save for or wait on a build. Codes protect you there too: working smoke alarms, safe wiring and proper egress are baseline requirements. If you are apartment hunting in the meantime, ask about flood history and storm preparation, and see our guide to <a href="/why-high-rise-apartments-in-houston-are-the-ultimate-living-experience/">high-rise living in Houston</a>.</p>

<h2>How to stay current</h2>
<ul>
<li>Read the adopted codes on the <a href="https://codes.iccsafe.org/codes/texas/houston-tx">Houston ICC building code site</a>.</li>
<li>Check local amendments, floodplain rules and permitting guidance with the Houston Permitting Center.</li>
<li>Work with licensed trades and a designer who builds in Houston regularly.</li>
<li>Keep photos of every stage before it is covered.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Building outside the City of Houston</h2>
<p>Greater Houston is a patchwork. Katy, Sugar Land, Pearland, The Woodlands area, Cypress and many other places you think of as Houston are separate cities, unincorporated county land, or areas served by municipal utility districts. In unincorporated Harris County, the county handles development and floodplain permitting rather than the city, and building oversight looks different than inside city limits. Fort Bend, Montgomery and Brazoria counties each have their own processes too. Confirm jurisdiction for your exact address before you assume Houston rules apply.</p>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Confirm your lot's flood zone and deed restrictions, then call the Houston Permitting Center about current code editions and fees. For the bigger picture, read our overview of <a href="/navigating-texas-building-codes-what-you-need-to-know/">Texas building codes</a> and our <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">new home construction timeline</a>, or start our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a>.</p>
